    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Gosnell, Arkansas, takes its name from Mervyn Gosnell, the eldest son of L.W., who helped establish the town’s first post office.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        2,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gosnell%2C_Arkansas",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Paragould and Southeast Railroad reached Gosnell before 1903, bringing the first train service to the community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        10,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gosnell%2C_Arkansas",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Traveling 1.3 miles from Blytheville on Gosnell Road leads to the Chickasawba Indian Mound, a notable prehistoric site.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://kids.kiddle.co/Gosnell,_Arkansas",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "By the mid‑1920s, Gosnell opened a one‑room schoolhouse that later expanded into a high school.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gosnell%2C_Arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In the early 1800s, Shawnee trader Chickasawba sold honey to riverboat passengers at Barfield Landing near Gosnell.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gosnell%2C_Arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The Eaker Site east of Gosnell once held a palisade, defensive trench, and temple mound.",
